findings.recommendation:
Make the fail-open contract structural rather than dependent on which exception fromisoformat happens to raise. Wrap the whole gate body:

    def is_dry_spell_ping_rate_limited() -> bool:
        try:
            conn = _connect()
            try:
                row = conn.execute(
                    "SELECT value FROM bot_state WHERE key=?",
                    (_KEY_DRY_SPELL_PINGED,),
                ).fetchone()
            finally:
                conn.close()
            if row is None:
                return False
            last = _parse_dt_tolerant(row[0], _KEY_DRY_SPELL_PINGED)
            ...
        except Exception:
            logger.warning(
                "[E017] rate-limit gate unreadable - alarm allowed through",
                exc_info=True,
            )
            return False

Additionally harden _parse_dt_tolerant itself (`except (ValueError, TypeError)`) so the same trap does not fire for the [E038] hold-cap gate and the soft-flag pair gate that share it. Extend the parametrised garbage list in test_unreadable_marker_does_not_suppress with a BLOB value written as sqlite3.Binary(b'...') so the non-str path is actually covered.

Decide the gate defensively so no DB condition can consume a detected alarm:

    try:
        rate_limited = pending_repo.is_dry_spell_ping_rate_limited()
    except Exception as gate_exc:
        logger.warning(
            f"[dry-spell] rate-limit gate unavailable, sending anyway: "
            f"{sanitize_error_message(gate_exc)}"
        )
        rate_limited = False

Symmetrically, mark_dry_spell_pinged() at news_bot.py:4995 raising after a successful send currently surfaces as '[dry-spell] channel-silence check failed', which misattributes a bookkeeping write failure to the check. Give it its own try/except with an accurate message so the log distinguishes 'alarm not sent' from 'alarm sent, marker not persisted'.

Normalise before comparing, and record the day rather than the instant:

    return last.astimezone(timezone.utc).date() == datetime.now(timezone.utc).date()

Stronger still, store the UTC date itself - datetime.now(timezone.utc).date().isoformat() - so the stored value states the unit the gate actually compares, removes the offset ambiguity for increments B and C, and closes the midnight mark-time window. Keep the tolerant parse for backward compatibility with any timestamp already written.
